The public health, safety, economy, good order, appearance, convenience, morals, and general welfare of the county require the harmonious, orderly and progressive use and development of land within the county. In furtherance of this general intent, the regulation of land use and development by the county is adopted to:
(1) Lessen congestion in the streets;
(2) Secure safety from fire, panic and other dangers;
(3) Promote the health and the general welfare;
(4) Provide adequate light and air;
(5) Prevent overcrowding of the land;
(6) Assure the timely provision of required streets, utilities, other facilities and services to new land development;
(7) Encourage land development in an orderly, serviceable and equitable manner;
(8) Protect property against blight and depreciation;
(9) Encourage the development of an economically stable community so as to help conserve and protect natural, economic, and scenic resources;
(10) Assure the provision of needed public open spaces and building sites in new land developments through the dedication or reservation of land for recreational, educational, and other public purposes;
(11) Encourage the most appropriate use of land and structures; and
(12) Assure the adequate provision of safe and convenient traffic access and circulation, both vehicular and pedestrian, in and through new land developments.
(Ord. No. 48, § 1-4, 1-9-2003)
